$fieldc2 = stripslashes('<h2 data-end=\"215\" data-start=\"173\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>INTER CARGO SERVICE</strong><br />
&nbsp;</h2>

<h3 data-end=\"215\" data-start=\"173\">✈️ <strong data-end=\"105\" data-start=\"85\">General Overview</strong></h3>

<p>Inter Cargo Service (ICS) was a French airline specialized in cargo and mail transport. Created in 1987 as a subsidiary of Air Inter, ICS responded to the growing demand for urgent mail and freight transport, especially after TAT Express failed to lease Airbus A300s for nighttime cargo flights. The airline primarily operated night flights in complement to Air Inter’s passenger services, with a fleet composed of Vickers Vanguard and Boeing 737 aircraft. ICS’s activities were later integrated into the Société d’exploitation aéropostale (SEA), which later became Europe Airpost and ASL Airlines France.</p>

<p data-end=\"1003\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\" data-start=\"837\"><strong data-end=\"847\" data-start=\"837\">Owner:</strong> Air Inter (55%) / Masurel Group (33%) / Paribas Bank (12%)<br data-end=\"912\" data-start=\"909\" />
<strong data-end=\"921\" data-start=\"912\">Type:</strong> Air freight and mail transport<br data-end=\"955\" data-start=\"952\" />
<strong data-end=\"968\" data-is-only-node=\"\" data-start=\"955\">Main Hub:</strong> Paris‑Orly<br data-end=\"982\" data-start=\"979\" />
<strong data-end=\"993\" data-start=\"982\">Period:</strong> 1987‑1990<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<h3 data-end=\"1293\" data-start=\"1154\">📜<strong>&nbsp;Origins and Creation</strong><strong data-end=\"251\" data-start=\"215\">&nbsp;(1986‑1987)</strong></h3>

<p data-end=\"1293\" data-start=\"1154\">Inter Cargo Service was established in late August 1986 to develop air cargo and mail transport in France. The airline was created by Air Inter to complement its transport services, with Gérard Perrin appointed as the first president. The decision to create ICS followed TAT Express’s unsuccessful attempt to operate nighttime cargo flights with Airbus A300s, leaving a gap in the urgent freight market. The company officially started operations in 1987 with an initial fleet of Vickers Vanguard and Boeing 737 aircraft, operating regular cargo and mail flights from its main hub at Paris‑Orly.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p data-end=\"1003\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\" data-start=\"837\"></p>

<h3 data-end=\"1384\" data-start=\"1346\">🛫 <strong data-end=\"1382\" data-start=\"1350\">Early Operations (1987‑1988)</strong></h3>

<p data-end=\"1584\" data-start=\"1385\">The <strong data-end=\"1407\" data-start=\"1389\">first aircraft</strong> operated by ICS were <strong data-end=\"1455\" data-start=\"1429\">Vickers Vanguard VC‑9s</strong> registered <em data-end=\"1475\" data-start=\"1467\">F‑GEJE</em> and <em data-end=\"1488\" data-start=\"1480\">F‑GEJF</em>.</p>

<p data-end=\"1829\" data-start=\"1586\">On <strong data-end=\"1609\" data-start=\"1589\">17 February 1987</strong>, ICS conducted its <strong data-end=\"1660\" data-start=\"1629\">first official cargo flight</strong> (flight ICS.1001) between <strong data-end=\"1717\" data-start=\"1687\">Paris‑Orly and Montpellier</strong> for TAT Express.</p>

<p data-end=\"2004\" data-start=\"1831\">In 1987, the young company transported approximately <strong data-end=\"1908\" data-start=\"1884\">68,000 tons of goods</strong>.</p>

<p data-end=\"2004\" data-start=\"1831\">By <strong data-end=\"2387\" data-start=\"2374\">late 1988</strong>, ICS introduced <strong data-end=\"2419\" data-start=\"2404\">Boeing 737s</strong> to operate night flights between <strong data-end=\"2493\" data-start=\"2453\">Paris‑Charles‑de‑Gaulle and Toulouse</strong> (two flights per night) and <strong data-end=\"2535\" data-start=\"2522\">Marseille</strong> (one flight per night).<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p data-end=\"2004\" data-start=\"1831\"></p>

<h3 data-end=\"3013\" data-start=\"2968\">🛩️ <strong data-end=\"3011\" data-start=\"2972\">Transition and Renaming (1990‑1991)</strong></h3>

<p data-end=\"3079\" data-start=\"3014\">As operations consolidated, <strong data-end=\"3076\" data-start=\"3042\">Intercargo Service was renamed</strong>:</p>

<ul>
	<li data-end=\"3241\" data-start=\"3083\">In <strong data-end=\"3094\" data-start=\"3086\">1990</strong>, the company became <strong data-end=\"3143\" data-start=\"3115\">Inter Ciel Service (ICS)</strong>.</li>
	<li data-end=\"3603\" data-start=\"3244\">On <strong data-end=\"3264\" data-start=\"3247\">22 March 1991</strong>, Air Inter, <strong data-end=\"3289\" data-start=\"3277\">La Poste</strong>, Air France, and <strong data-end=\"3314\" data-start=\"3307\">TAT</strong> merged efforts to create <strong data-end=\"3384\" data-start=\"3340\">Société d’exploitation aéropostale (SEA)</strong>, the direct successor of Intercargo Service. This new structure took over cargo and postal operations in France and Europe.</li>
</ul>

<p data-end=\"3725\" data-start=\"3605\">Under the Société d’exploitation aéropostale, aircraft — often <strong data-end=\"3700\" data-start=\"3668\">Boeing 737 Quick Change (QC)</strong> — had a <strong data-end=\"3722\" data-start=\"3709\">dual role</strong>:</p>

<ul>
	<li data-end=\"3829\" data-start=\"3728\">Transport passengers <strong data-end=\"3767\" data-start=\"3749\">during the day</strong> under Air France, Air Inter, or Air Charter flight numbers;</li>
	<li data-end=\"4071\" data-start=\"3832\">Be converted into <strong data-end=\"3882\" data-start=\"3850\">cargo configuration at night</strong> for operators such as <strong data-end=\"3917\" data-start=\"3905\">La Poste</strong>, <strong data-end=\"3933\" data-start=\"3919\">Chronopost</strong>, or sometimes newspapers like <em data-end=\"3975\" data-start=\"3964\">Le Figaro</em>.<br />
	&nbsp;</li>
</ul>

<p data-end=\"4071\" data-start=\"3832\"></p>

<h3 data-end=\"4147\" data-start=\"4078\">🛑<strong data-end=\"4145\" data-start=\"4082\">After 1991 – Towards Europe Airpost and ASL Airlines France</strong></h3>

<p data-end=\"4192\" data-start=\"4148\">Later developments led to further changes:</p>

<p data-end=\"4467\" data-start=\"4194\">📌 In <strong data-end=\"4208\" data-start=\"4200\">1995</strong>, the ownership was restructured: Air France and <strong data-end=\"4269\" data-start=\"4257\">La Poste</strong> became equal shareholders, with La Poste represented by <strong data-end=\"4338\" data-start=\"4326\">Sofipost</strong> (40 %) and <strong data-end=\"4364\" data-start=\"4350\">Chronopost</strong> (10 %).</p>

<p data-end=\"4684\" data-start=\"4469\">📌 In <strong data-end=\"4483\" data-start=\"4475\">2000</strong>, the company was renamed <strong data-end=\"4527\" data-start=\"4509\">Europe Airpost</strong>, operating <strong data-end=\"4569\" data-start=\"4539\">Airbus A300 cargo aircraft</strong> and ATR 72s.</p>

<p data-end=\"4901\" data-start=\"4686\">📌 In <strong data-end=\"4700\" data-start=\"4692\">2008</strong>, the <strong data-end=\"4730\" data-start=\"4706\">ASL Aviation Ireland</strong> group acquired Europe Airpost, continuing postal and cargo services.</p>

<p data-end=\"5110\" data-start=\"4903\">📌 Finally, in <strong data-end=\"4926\" data-start=\"4918\">2015</strong>, Europe Airpost was renamed <strong data-end=\"4978\" data-start=\"4955\">ASL Airlines France</strong> under the ASL Aviation group.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p></p>

